Write a reusable Python function solve(givens) for the geometry task below. The input is a dictionary of real-valued numerical givens; the output must be one finite real number for the requested quantity. It must work for every nondegenerate configuration satisfying the stated assumptions, including new numerical conditions. Derive a procedure from the geometry; do not hard-code a table of examples.

Let H be the orthocenter, M the midpoint of BC, and Ω the circumcircle of an acute scalene triangle ABC. Line AB meets the circle centered at C with radius CA again at D, and line AC meets the circle centered at B with radius BA again at E (other than A). Rays MD and ME meet Ω at P and Q, respectively. Let R be the common point other than M of circles BPM and CQM. If AH=givens["AH"] and AR=givens["AR"], find the area of triangle AHR. Assume all circles and intersections are nondegenerate.

The dictionary has exactly these fields: AH, AR. Inputs and reference values use ordinary floating-point precision. Accuracy is checked with absolute tolerance 1e-7 plus relative tolerance 1e-6.
You may use ordinary Python and these standard numerical modules: math, cmath, statistics, fractions, decimal, itertools, functools. You have no geometry solver, external data, files, network, or other packages. General numerical algorithms and ordinary control flow are permitted. Do not use reflection, double-underscore attributes, eval, exec, or dynamic imports.
Return one closed Python code block containing the complete program and a top-level def solve(givens). Do not print results or read input; the evaluator calls solve. Return code only, without comments, docstrings, proofs or prose.

Observed numerical examples (not an exhaustive test set):
